A||||2||mbramble||'87 2.5L Motor/Tranny  Mount Questions||||1||
Z||000000||mbramble||09-28-2000||09:40 PM||||Picked up a new dogbone at AutoZone but they don't carry the motor mount.  Went to a DAPS near my house and checked with them.  For '87 they show a front and rear tranny mount but no motor mount.  Reading Ogre's info (and my Hayne's) about the tranny mount, there should be only one tranny mount.  All the differnt parts houses I called showed basically the same thing.  Pontiac dealer can order a motor mount for approx $90!<P>They do have a motor mount listed for an '85.  Is it the same??  The drawing in the Haynes manual looks like what they showed me for an '85.  <P>What transmission mount do I get when they show a front and rear one for '87???<P>Thanks,<BR>Mike<P>Guess I neglected to mention that I have an auto tranny.  Maybe the parts houses I contacted aren't showing the info for the auto tranny, and only list the two mounts for the manual??<p>[This message has been edited by mbramble (edited 09-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||WKDFIRO||09-29-2000||12:22 AM||||You didn't say (or I didn't see) but Im going to bet that you have an 87 Automatic 4 cylinder. If this is so than you have essentially 3 mounts on the powertrain. The dog bone (torque strut), a single tranny mount that holds the tranny by way of a bracket with  3 "fingers," and the engine mount which can be found below the main pulley crank. (The V6 has 2 mounts for the tranny, the engine mount and the torque strut) The $90 mount that you got quoted is unfortunately the one you need. It is a fluid filled engine mount for the 4 cylinder engines to lessen the vibrations. This also means that its a dealer only item. (Unless someone has found them someplace else!) The other motor mount (found on the V6) almost but not quite fits in the holes. The holes can be gouged out a bit to fit but when I was in the predicament I declined to experiment. The single tranny mount, like the motor mount is roughly in an H configuration. When I rebuilt the engine I swiped the mounts from a V6 Automatic and replaced the single mount. It did increase vibration slightly (in addition to the urethane dog bone) but will allow more access to the cover for the tranny on the back end. <BR>      The problem with the engine mount is that when the oil filter is changed the excess oil spills down the C-bracket that the engine mount is bolted to. The oil seems to goe straight down and pools up in the engine mount. The next time I change this mount Im thinking of having some sort of shield of sheet metal to try to divert the oil from pooling.<BR>        This, in time wears down the mount and causes serious problems. If the mount were to be completely destroyed the bracket on the dogbone connected to the engine will crack and eventually break, in your current mount configuration you will then have an engine that flops around like a fish in the back. We saw one that had dented the air filter and was punching the dogbone into the valve cover when a gear was selected. Brutal stuff!<P>Hope this helps!<BR><p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 09-29-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||DjKid||09-29-2000||02:31 AM||||I got my at Advance... here in town, they had one in stock.. 23.94$  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> mines an 88 but it doesn't look any differant? 90$ is just too much.<P>------------------<BR>Ray - Blue 88 Coupe - <A HREF="http://blueknight.carolinafieros.com" TARGET=_blank>My Blue Knight</A> - <A HREF="http://www.carolinafieros.com" TARGET=_blank> Carolina Fieros</A>||||reg||1
